@* AppShell's Width and scrolling page, moved from its Overview: a reading column and desktop-app
   scrolling, each a page of its own in a frame with a phone width. *@

<Heading Level="2">A reading column</Heading>
<TextBlock>A reading column centers the content of the header, the main area and the footer on one narrow column, as for articles. The bars and their lines stay full width. Set <Code>ContentWidth</Code> on the shell, such as <Code>Length.Rem(42)</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="app-shell-reading" WidthSwitch />
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem>A part's own <Code>ContentWidth</Code> overrides the shell's, such as for a wider header over a narrow article.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>A <Anchor Href="docs/components/section">Section</Anchor> in the main area still spans the whole area, for a band of color from edge to edge.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Scrolling like a desktop app</Heading>
<TextBlock>A shell can scroll like a desktop app: it fills the window, and only the main area scrolls, so the header, the sidebar and the footer never move. Set <Code>ScrollMode="ShellScroll.Main"</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="app-shell-inbox" WidthSwitch />
<TextBlock>With the default, <Code>ShellScroll.Page</Code>, the window scrolls as on any web page. The header stays at the top, and the content of the sidebar and the aside stays in view beside the page.</TextBlock>
